{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/evidence-ledger-across-demonstrations.md", "content": "# Evidence ledger across ARC demonstrations

When a recurring ARC task presents multiple demonstrations with changing colors, orientations, or boundary layouts, keep one cumulative ledger rather than inferring each example in isolation.

For each demo, compare input/output cell-by-cell and record:
- all changed coordinates and old/new values;
- any long edge stripe, short marker, compact prototype, or special region;
- exact alignment/orientation and the relation between structures;
- where changes start and stop, including marker endpoint treatment;
- same-looking but unchanged cells/components as negative evidence.

Then test a single unified hypothesis against the *complete* difference set of every demo. Demos may illustrate different orientations or palette mappings of one rule; apparent local patterns can be misleading if they are treated as separate rules. Construct test predictions for each remaining candidate and request another demo only if it distinguishes them. After any incorrect answer, reopen the whole hypothesis, not merely the changed coordinates in the last output. Do not submit another plausible-looking guess until it fits all available examples.

This procedure is a general safeguard learned from a difficult boundary-stripe/marker instance where several individually plausible but inconsistent interpretations led to repeated incorrect submissions. It does not assert a task-specific transformation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792485072/attempts/fdab98880f7040698d48fca3d3e4aab1/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzles/continual-arc-grid-reasoning/references/evidence-ledger-across-demonstrations.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/demo-request-economics.md", "content": "# Demo request economics and discipline

In ARC stream tasks, a demo request and a wrong submission both have an explicit cost, while an instance that ends unsolved can incur the full attempt budget. Avoid reflexively requesting examples: first record the unresolved ambiguity and at least two candidate rules, then spell out how their predicted test outputs differ. Ask for another demo only if it can discriminate those alternatives and change the decision. After each response, update the hypothesis from changed and unchanged cells; do not repeat a request without a new discriminating question. If the evidence does not uniquely settle the rule, weigh the expected value of another demo against its fixed cost and the risk of a wrong submission. Keep the user-facing ARC protocol exact: emit one action JSON only.

A practical safeguard: maintain a compact change ledger (coordinate, old value, new value, component/shape, nearby unchanged counterexamples) before generalizing from examples. In particular, a single transformed object among visually similar objects means the untouched near-match is evidence that the rule is selective; explicitly characterize that distinction before applying the rule to a fresh grid.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792485072/attempts/fdab98880f7040698d48fca3d3e4aab1/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzles/continual-arc-grid-reasoning/references/demo-request-economics.md"}

